const GR_P7A=[
 ["Potential form","Verb potential form (食べられる, 飲める)","The potential form says “can do”. For る-verbs, take off る and add られる: 食べる becomes 食べられる, 見る becomes 見られる. For other verbs, change the last sound to the matching え-row sound and add る: 飲む becomes 飲める, 行く becomes 行ける, 泳ぐ becomes 泳げる. Two verbs are irregular: する becomes できる and 来る becomes 来られる. The thing you can do usually takes が instead of を: 日本語が話せます means “I can speak Japanese”.",[["私","watashi","I"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["日本語","nihongo","Japanese"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["話せます","hanasemasu","can speak"],["。","",""]],"I can speak Japanese."],
 ["Can be seen or heard with 見える and 聞こえる","Thing が 見える／聞こえる","見える (mieru) means that something can be seen without effort, because it is in view. 聞こえる (kikoeru) means that a sound can be heard without effort, because it reaches your ears. The thing that is seen or heard takes が. Compare 見られる: it means that you are able to see something because you have a chance, for example a ticket. 山が見えます means “A mountain can be seen”.",[["窓","mado","window"],["から","kara","from"],["山","yama","mountain"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["見えます","miemasu","can be seen"],["。","",""]],"A mountain can be seen from the window."],
 ["Passive form","Person は + (doer に) + verb passive form (れる, られる)","The passive form says that something is done to the subject, or that something is done without naming the person who does it. For る-verbs, take off る and add られる: 決める becomes 決められる. For other verbs, change the last sound to the matching あ-row sound and add れる: 運ぶ becomes 運ばれる, 呼ぶ becomes 呼ばれる. For verbs that end in う, use わ: 言う becomes 言われる. する becomes される and 来る becomes 来られる. The person who does the action is marked with に: 先生に呼ばれました means “I was called by the teacher”.",[["私","watashi","I"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["先生","sensei","teacher"],["に","ni","by"],["呼ばれました","yobaremashita","was called"],["。","",""]],"I was called by the teacher."],
 ["Causative form","Person に or を + verb causative form (させる)","The causative form says that one person makes or lets another person do something. The situation shows which meaning is used. For る-verbs, take off る and add させる: 食べる becomes 食べさせる. For other verbs, change the last sound to the matching あ-row sound and add せる: 走る becomes 走らせる, 習う becomes 習わせる. する becomes させる and 来る becomes 来させる. The person who does the action takes に when the verb has an object, and を when the verb has no object: 弟に空手を習わせます, 弟を走らせます.",[["コーチ","koochi","coach"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["選手","senshu","players"],["を","o","(object)"],["走らせます","hashirasemasu","makes run"],["。","",""]],"The coach makes the players run."],
 ["Asking permission with させてください","Verb causative て-form + ください","Make the causative form, change it to the て-form and add ください. The て-form of させる is させて: 見学する becomes 見学させてください, and 食べる becomes 食べさせてください. It means “Please let me do it”. The speaker is the person who does the action. Use it to ask a person who can decide, for example a teacher or a shop clerk.",[["ここ","koko","here"],["で","de","at"],["練習","renshuu","practice"],["させてください","sasetekudasai","please let me do"],["。","",""]],"Please let me practise here."]
];
