const GR_P9B=[
 ["Reporting a request with ように言う","Verb + ように言いました","ように (you ni) before 言いました reports what someone asked or told another person to do. Use the dictionary form for “do”: 来るように言いました means “told (someone) to come”. Use the ない-form for “do not”: 来ないように言いました means “told (someone) not to come”. The person who is told is marked with に. This is different from the quotation with と, which repeats the exact words.",[["先生","sensei","teacher"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["私","watashi","I"],["に","ni","to"],["早く","hayaku","early"],["来る","kuru","come"],["ように","you ni","so that, to"],["言いました","iimashita","said"],["。","",""]],"The teacher told me to come early."],
 ["Commands and prohibition","Verb command form; verb dictionary form + な","The command form is short and strong. For る-verbs, change る to ろ: 食べる becomes 食べろ. For other verbs, change the last sound to the え-row sound: 守る becomes 守れ and 行く becomes 行け. する becomes しろ and 来る becomes 来い. To forbid, put な after the dictionary form: 走るな means “do not run”. These forms sound rough in conversation. They appear on signs, in sports and between close friends. To other people, use ください or ないでください.",[["ここ","koko","here"],["で","de","at"],["走る","hashiru","to run"],["な","na","(links to noun)"],["。","",""]],"Do not run here."],
 ["Casual speech with plain form","Plain form at the end of a sentence","With friends and family, use the plain form at the end of a sentence. です becomes だ. A ます-verb becomes the dictionary form: 行きます becomes 行く. ました becomes the た-form: 行きました becomes 行った. ません becomes the ない-form: 行きません becomes 行かない. For a question, leave out か and raise your voice. Do not use the plain form to a teacher, a customer or a person you meet for the first time.",[["明日","ashita","tomorrow"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["休み","yasumi","holiday, day off"],["だ","da","is"],["。","",""]],"Tomorrow is a holiday."],
 ["Very polite with ございます","Noun + でございます; あります becomes ございます","Shop staff and hotel staff use very polite words with customers. でございます (de gozaimasu) is the very polite form of です. ございます (gozaimasu) is the very polite form of あります. メニューがございます means “there is a menu”. ありがとうございます is a very polite “thank you”. Use these forms for things and situations in your work. Do not use them for yourself in daily life.",[["メニュー","menyuu","menu"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["ここ","koko","here"],["に","ni","to"],["ございます","gozaimasu","there is (very polite)"],["。","",""]],"The menu is here."],
 ["Asking politely with でしょうか and ませんか","Sentence + でしょうか","Replace ですか with でしょうか (deshou ka) to ask a question more politely. It sounds softer. Use it with customers, guests and people you do not know. ませんか (masen ka) is also polite: 食べませんか means “Would you like to eat?”. Use it to invite a person or to ask a person politely to do something.",[["駅","eki","station"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["どこ","doko","where"],["でしょう","deshou","probably is"],["か","ka","(question)"],["。","",""]],"Where is the station?"]
];
