const GR_P10A=[
 ["Because of something bad with せいで","Plain form + せいで, bad result","せいで (seide) after a plain form gives the cause of a bad result. The plain form is the short form of a verb or adjective, for example 降った (fell) and うるさい (noisy). After a noun, use の: 工事のせいで. After a な-adjective, use な. The cause is bad and the result is bad. The result is written after a comma.",[["工事","kouji","construction work"],["の","no","(of)"],["音","oto","sound"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["うるさい","urusai","noisy"],["せいで","seide","because of"],["頭","atama","head"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["痛い","itai","painful"],["です","desu","is"],["。","",""]],"Because the noise from the construction work is loud, my head hurts."],
 ["Thanks to with おかげで","Plain form + おかげで, good result","おかげで (okage de) after a plain form gives the cause of a good result. It shows thanks. After a noun, use の: 先生のおかげで. After a な-adjective, use な. The result is good and is written after a comma.",[["先生","sensei","teacher"],["に","ni","from"],["習った","naratta","learned"],["おかげで","okage de","thanks to"],["日本語","nihongo","Japanese"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["分かります","wakarimasu","understand"],["。","",""]],"Thanks to learning from the teacher, I understand Japanese."],
 ["That is why with わけです","Plain form + わけです","わけです (wake desu) after a plain form says that the earlier fact is the reason, and so the result is natural: “that is why”. After a noun, use の. After a な-adjective, use な. The reason is often written before the word から.",[["毎日","mainichi","every day"],["練習している","renshuu shite iru","practise"],["から","kara","because"],["上手な","jouzu na","skilful"],["わけです","wake desu","that is why"],["。","",""]],"Because he practises every day, that is why he is skilful."],
 ["It does not mean that with わけではありません","Plain form + わけではありません","わけではありません (wake dewa arimasen) after a plain form means “it does not mean that …”. It denies a conclusion that a listener may draw. After a noun, use の. After a な-adjective, use な.",[["毎日","mainichi","every day"],["忙しい","isogashii","busy"],["わけではありません","wake dewa arimasen","it is not the case that"],["。","",""]],"It is not the case that I am busy every day."],
 ["Cannot be with はずがありません","Plain form + はずがありません","はずがありません (hazu ga arimasen) after a plain form means that the speaker is sure that something cannot be true: “it cannot be that …”. It is a strong opinion. After a noun, use の. After a な-adjective, use な.",[["友達","tomodachi","friend"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["知っている","shitte iru","knows"],["はずがありません","hazu ga arimasen","cannot be"],["。","",""]],"My friend cannot possibly know."]
];
