const GR_P11A=[
 ["Just did with たばかりです","Verb た-form + ばかりです","たばかり (ta bakari) after the た-form of a verb means that the action ended a short time ago: “just did”. The た-form of 食べる is 食べた. The た-form of 飲む is 飲んだ. 食べたばかりです means “I have just eaten”. The speaker feels that the time is short. It can be minutes or months. For する-words, write 入社したばかりです.",[["昼ご飯","hirugohan","lunch"],["を","o","(object)"],["食べたばかり","tabeta bakari","just ate"],["です","desu","is"],["。","",""]],"I have just eaten lunch."],
 ["Only doing with ばかり","Noun or verb て-form + ばかり","ばかり (bakari) after a noun means “only”, and the speaker feels that it is too much: 肉ばかり食べます means “I eat only meat, nothing else”. After the て-form of a verb, add います: 寝てばかりいます means “I only sleep, again and again”. This is different from だけ (only), which has no such feeling.",[["弟","otouto","younger brother"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["ゲーム","geemu","games"],["ばかり","bakari","only"],["して","shite","doing"],["います","imasu","is"],["。","",""]],"My younger brother does nothing but play games."],
 ["While it is still so with うちに","Plain form + うちに, action","うちに (uchi ni) means “while a state lasts”. The state is expected to change, so you do the action before that. 若いうちに旅行します means “I will travel while I am young”. After a noun, use の: 子供のうちに. After a な-adjective, use な: 元気なうちに. After a verb or い-adjective, use the plain form.",[["若い","wakai","young"],["うちに","uchi ni","while"],["旅行","ryokou","travel"],["します","shimasu","do"],["。","",""]],"I will travel while I am young."],
 ["Leaving as it is with まま","Verb た-form or noun + まま","まま (mama) means that a state stays the same and another action happens in that state. 電気をつけたまま寝ました means “I slept with the light left on”. After a noun, use の: 靴のまま入ります means “I enter with my shoes on”.",[["窓","mado","window"],["を","o","(object)"],["開けた","aketa","opened"],["まま","mama","as it is"],["出かけました","dekakemashita","went out"],["。","",""]],"I went out with the window left open."],
 ["Without doing with ずに","Verb ない-form − ない + ずに","ずに (zu ni) means “without doing”, like ないで. Take the ない-form and remove ない, then add ずに: 食べない becomes 食べずに, 行かない becomes 行かずに. The word する is irregular: せずに. 食べずに行きます means “I go without eating”.",[["朝ご飯","asagohan","breakfast"],["を","o","(object)"],["食べ","tabe","eat"],["ずに","zu ni","without"],["会社","kaisha","company"],["に","ni","to"],["行きました","ikimashita","went"],["。","",""]],"I went to the company without eating breakfast."]
];
