const GR_P11B=[
 ["The moment with とたんに","Verb た-form + とたんに","とたんに (totan ni) after the た-form of a verb means “the moment that happened, something else happened”. The second event is sudden and is not planned. 家を出たとたんに雨が降りました means “The moment I left the house, it started to rain”. Do not use it for requests or for the speaker’s own plan.",[["家","ie","home"],["を","o","(object)"],["出た","deta","left"],["とたんに","totan ni","the moment"],["雨","ame","rain"],["が","ga","(subject)"],["降りました","furimashita","fell"],["。","",""]],"The moment I left the house, it started to rain."],
 ["Trying to with ようとする","Verb volitional form + とする","The volitional form is the form used for “let us”. For る-verbs, take off る and add よう: 食べる becomes 食べよう. For other verbs, change the last sound to the matching お-row sound and add う: 行く becomes 行こう, 乗る becomes 乗ろう. For する it is しよう, and for 来る it is こよう. Add とする (to suru) to say that you try to do it or are about to do it: 乗ろうとしました means “I tried to get on”.",[["電車","densha","train"],["に","ni","onto"],["乗ろう","norou","(volitional of 乗る)"],["と","to","(quote)"],["しました","shimashita","did"],["。","",""]],"I tried to get on the train."],
 ["While doing something else with ついでに","Verb dictionary or た-form + ついでに","ついでに (tsuide ni) means that while you do one action, you also do a second small action at the same place or time. 買い物に行くついでに、手紙を出します means “While I go shopping, I will also post a letter”. After a noun, use の: 散歩のついでに. The first action is the main one.",[["買い物","kaimono","shopping"],["に","ni","for"],["行く","iku","go"],["ついでに","tsuide ni","while at it"],["、","",""],["手紙","tegami","letter"],["を","o","(object)"],["出します","dashimasu","post"],["。","",""]],"While going shopping, I will also post a letter."],
 ["Every time with たびに","Dictionary form or noun の + たびに","たびに (tabi ni) means “every time that happens”. After a verb, use the dictionary form: 会うたびに. After a noun, use の: 旅行のたびに. The second part tells what always happens at that time. 彼に会うたびに、嬉しいです means “Every time I meet him, I am happy”.",[["彼","kare","he"],["に","ni","(with)"],["会う","au","meet"],["たびに","tabi ni","every time"],["、","",""],["嬉しい","ureshii","happy"],["です","desu","am"],["。","",""]],"Every time I meet him, I am happy."],
 ["Even while with ながらも","Verb stem + ながらも","ながらも (nagara mo) after a verb stem means “even while”, and the two parts are in contrast. It is more formal than ながら. 疲れながらも、勉強を続けます means “Even while tired, I continue studying”. It can also follow い-adjectives and nouns in a few set phrases. The main idea is in the second part.",[["疲れ","tsukare","get tired"],["ながらも","nagara mo","even while"],["、","",""],["勉強","benkyou","study"],["を","o","(object)"],["続けます","tsudzukemasu","continue"],["。","",""]],"Even while tired, I continue studying."]
];
