const GR_P13A=[
 ["Being made to do with causative passive","Verb causative passive form (食べさせられる)","The causative passive form means that someone makes you do something, often something you do not want to do. For る-verbs, take off る and add させられる: 食べる becomes 食べさせられる. For other verbs, change the last sound to the matching あ-row sound and add される: 描く becomes 描かされる, 飲む becomes 飲まされる. する becomes させられる. 来る becomes こさせられる. The person who makes you do it is marked with に. 母に野菜を食べさせられました means “My mother made me eat vegetables”.",[["母","haha","my mother"],["に","ni","to"],["野菜","yasai","vegetables"],["を","o","(object)"],["食べさせられました","tabesaseraremashita","was made to eat"],["。","",""]],"I was made to eat vegetables by my mother."],
 ["Should with べきです","Dictionary form + べきです","べきです (beki desu) after the dictionary form of a verb says that something is right to do, or that a person ought to do it: “should”. 勉強するべきです means “should study”. For する, both するべき and すべき are used. This is stronger than ほうがいいです.",[["学生","gakusei","student"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["勉強","benkyou","study"],["する","suru","to do"],["べきです","bekidesu","should"],["。","",""]],"Students should study."],
 ["General truth with ものです","Plain form + ものです","ものです (mono desu) after a plain form says that something is usually true or is the nature of people or things. It is often used for feelings and for how people behave. 泣くものです means “people tend to cry”.",[["子供","kodomo","child"],["は","wa","(topic)"],["よく","yoku","often"],["泣く","naku","to cry"],["ものです","monodesu","tend to"],["。","",""]],"Children often cry."],
 ["Advice with ことです","Dictionary form or ない-form + ことです","ことです (koto desu) after the dictionary form or the ない-form of a verb gives firm advice: “what you should do is …”. Teachers and doctors use it. 練習することです means “what you should do is practise”. 忘れないことです means “what you should do is not forget”.",[["毎日","mainichi","every day"],["練習","renshuu","practice"],["する","suru","to do"],["ことです","kotodesu","what you should do is"],["。","",""]],"What you should do is practise every day."],
 ["Cannot stand it with てたまらない","て-form or adjective + てたまらない","たまらない (tamaranai) after the て-form of a word means that a feeling or a body state is too strong to bear: “cannot stand it”. For an い-adjective, change い to くて: 眠い becomes 眠くて. For a verb with たい, use たくて: 行きたくて. For a な-adjective or a noun, use で: 心配で. The polite form is たまりません.",[["眠くて","nemukute","sleepy (て-form)"],["たまりません","tamarimasen","cannot stand it"],["。","",""]],"I am so sleepy that I cannot stand it."]
];
